 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. Eating out and takeaway in Melbourne cost 3.5% more in August 2026 than a year earlier. That's the latest ABS CPI figure for \"meals out and take away foods\".\n\n- **Recent trend:** The annual rate has stayed between about 3.3% and 4.4% since mid-2025. It peaked at 4.4% in May 2026 and was 4.1% in July 2026.\n- **Compared with Australia:** The national rate was 4.1% in August 2026, so Melbourne is a little lower. The national rate has risen from 3.0% in April 2025.\n- **Earlier years:** Melbourne's annual rate was 5.8% in March quarter 2024. It slowed to 2.3% by June quarter 2025 before picking up again.\n- **Price level:** The index rose from 95.4 in April 2024 to 103.29 in August 2026 (September 2025 = 100). That's about 8% over roughly two and a quarter years.\n- **Longer run:** On the older quarterly series, a separate index with a different base, prices rose from 122.1 in March quarter 2021 to 150.4 in September quarter 2025. That's about 23% in four and a half years.\n\nPrices are still rising faster than the RBA's 2–3% inflation target range, though the rise in Melbourne is slower than in the 2022–23 surge.",
